-- title:
--   Cyclotomic descent of fine moduli for polarised abelian schemes
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $g$, $d$, $n$ and a predicate $Q$ assigning to every commutative ring $S$ a property of objects of `PolarisedAbelianScheme g d n S` (a scheme $A$ over $\operatorname{Spec} S$ with a commutative relative group law, the abelian-scheme property bundle, all fibres of topological Krull dimension $g$, a basis $P_0,\dots,P_{2g-1}$ of $n$-torsion sections in the sense that on every algebraically closed geometric fibre the $n$-torsion is freely generated by the $P_i$, and an invertible module `pol` admitting a closed immersion by sections with geometric fibre $H^0$-rank $d$). Assume $Q$ is stable under base change, i.e. $Q\,S\,u$ and `PolarisedAbelianScheme.IsPullback` $\varphi\,u\,u'$ for a ring map $\varphi : S \to S'$ imply $Q\,S'\,u'$; and that $Q$ descends along faithfully flat étale extensions $S \to S'$: if $u'$ is the pullback of $u$ and $Q\,S'\,u'$ holds, then so does $Q\,S\,u$. Assume $3 \le n$, let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring in which the images of $n$ and of some $m > 0$ are units, and assume the local representability hypothesis: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ and every $\zeta \in B$ with $\zeta^m = 1$ and $1 - \zeta^j$ a unit for all $0 < j < m$, there are a scheme $M'$, a morphism $\pi_{M'} : M' \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ and an assignment $\mathrm{pt}'$ sending each commutative ring $S$, each $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ and each pair $(u, \text{proof of } Q\,S\,u)$ to a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} S \to M'$ over $s$, such that $\mathrm{pt}'$ is a fine moduli datum (constant on isomorphism classes, compatible with pullbacks along ring maps, surjective onto $s$-morphisms, and injective up to isomorphism), and $\pi_{M'}$ is separated, quasi-compact and locally of finite presentation, every finite set of points of $M'$ lies in a single affine open, and $M'$ admits an immersion into $\operatorname{Proj}$ of the polynomial ring in $qpn+1$ variables over $B$ compatible with the projection to $\operatorname{Spec} B$. The conclusion is that all of this holds over $\mathcal O$ itself: there exist $M$, $\pi_M : M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and $\mathrm{pt}$ with `Satisfying.IsFineModuli`, $\pi_M$ separated, quasi-compact and locally of finite presentation, the affine-covering property for finite subsets of $M$, and an immersion of $M$ into a projective space over $\mathcal O$ compatible with $\pi_M$.
--
--   This is the descent step in the construction of moduli schemes for polarised abelian schemes with level structure: representability by a quasi-projective fine moduli scheme over a base in which a suitably non-degenerate $m$-th root of unity is available is propagated to the base $\mathcal O$ itself, provided $m$ is invertible there. It feeds the construction of the moduli scheme for the theta-type moduli problem over a general base.
